Z||000036||stimpy||04-13-2001||01:59 PM||||At the risk of being re-branded as a bleeding heart, leftist commie tree hugging pinko radical, I would like to clarify my viewpoint. I think events like Cincinatti and the Rodney King trial riots come far short of being civil rights protests. I think a lot of it is people taking advantage of a feeling of lawlessness due to disrespect of authority. In situations like this, the disrespect comes from a history of injustice that cannot be denied. Sure, you can isolate Amadou Diallo or this kid in Cincinatti and justify the death, but 200+ years of brutality and systematic repression of minorities is hard to turn a blind eye towards if you are a minority. That history is a prime trigger towards taking part in that type of lawlessness.  Do I feel that that excuses this behavior? Absolutely not!  But if we as a society wish to prevent this in the future, then we must recognize what causes this and take steps to remedy those social ills.  <P>Another thought log I'd like to throw on the fire is why is non-lethal weapons not more prevelently used by police. Here in OKC, there have been at least 5 individuals shot to death by police when the suspects were armed with weapons like shovels, machetes, and kitchen knives.  These were not close-range hand to hand combat situations, but long stand-offs culminating in the shootings.  Not really related to the Ohio issue, just a pondering.||||reg||1
