Z||000055||tgowens||04-16-2001||08:49 PM||||well........this <I>is</I> an interesting subject........<P>I've been reading along here for a while and have a thing or two to say.<P>First, everyone is entitled to their opinion and we have sure heard the full spectrum here.<P>Second, I lived in Cincinnati for 15 years and although Cincinnati <I>does</I> have a lot to offer, it is not without its faults. I believe )and this is <B>my</B> opinion......that there <I>is</I> an amount of corruption in Cincinnati. There are law enforcement agencies run more like military units and taking (not necessarily <I>given</I>) more authority than they are empowered by law. It is a fact that each law enforcement official in Cincinnati will "stick up for their own" whether right or wrong - and I can attest to this first hand as I was a victim of a corrupted law enforcement officer and witnessed just how the court system will stand behind their man regardless of the facts presented. Like I said - I witnessed this first hand. Cincinnati, in my opinion, is NOT the greatest place in the world.<P>Third, Cincinnati is also the city that has had court battle after court battle with Larry Flynt (of Hustler Magazine fame) (in fact, the Prosecutor in that case is now the Sheriff)- not that I agree with Mr. Flynt's type of business or operation I <I>do</I> feel that he has a right to free enterprise just as anyone else in this country. Now, if the obscenity laws prevent him from doing certain things in his business and he follows those laws, he should be left to run that business. Such is/was not the case in Cincinnati.<P>Fourth, Cincinnati was the city which banned the Robert Maplethorpe exhibit at the Arts Center because of a few photographs depicting two men together. While this exhibit toured the country, it was conservative, predominately Catholic, predominately German Cincinnati that banned the exhibit - not willing to have the offensive photographs removed from the exhibit - they banned the whole thing!<P>Fifth, this is also the city that had as its Mayor one Jerry Springer who not only hired a prostitute while in office but paid her with a check that bounced!!<P>So you see, Cincinnati is not without its faults and certainly suffers from the stresses and pressures that every city of its size does but, I feel that the very nature of this particular incident just brought to a head what has been brewing in Cincinnati for a long long time.<P>Now, Blacks, Whites, Chinese, Mexican - doesn't matter - there are good and bad in any race -- this was simply an incident that has, once again, been used as an example and blown out of proportion and gained media play in order to try to lend credence to whichever side feels that they need it. <P>Personally, I feel that there will be a Federal investigation, time will pass, and it will simply die out with no actual end result - just as it has every other time it has happened in Cincinnati.<P>The aforementioned are the opinions of this writer and not necessarily the views of this station.............<p>[This message has been edited by tgowens (edited 04-17-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000056||BAM-BAM||04-17-2001||01:23 AM|||| A 36 year old french tourist was beaten to death in the Clark County Detention center, A 28 year old black man with Meth on his person carrying a basketball was shoot 14 times when he reach for his jar as it slid down his leg, a 34, year old retarted hispanic man was shot eight times and killed while standing in his driveway with a hunting knife, (surrounded by police). A black man was shot and killed by police while reaching for his wallet, a black man was beaten severely for breaking probation and driving under the influence, What's the common denominator in all this. Police and protection agencies are to quick to react in a violent manner because there is no governing agency to regulate them. They are self regulated and that never works. Try alowing a group of highschool seniors the right to self regulate their own on campus behavior, (mayhem). I don't own, have never owned, nor will I own a weapon. If I am so lucky as to catch someone breaking into my home, I have contingencies in place to deal with that. If you are running off my property, I have done my job. I do pitty the fool that ever harms one of mine. I was a militarily trained marksman good to 500 meters. You can run, but you can't hide. I hope life never leads me down that road. I don't hunt to kill, I don't antagonize to incite, I judge everyone on their own merits and charactor. I have many friend of diverse ethnic backgrounds, but in the end we are all humans. I don't know any "Black" men or "hispanic" men or "Asian" men Or "Caucasion" men. I know who my brothers are and they know me. If I meet you on the street, just treat me as you want to be treated and we will get along fine. Respect is a two way street, "You get what you give and you give what you get" <P><BR>Peace, B2 OUT! Love ya all!!! <P>------------------<BR>Knowledge is POWER!<BR>That is why the ignorant remain Weak!||||reg||1
Z||000057||tgowens||04-18-2001||01:56 AM||||well said B2.......well said!||||reg||1
Z||000058||Taijiguy||04-19-2001||09:24 AM||||Ugh....can't believe I'm jumping into this mess. I have to disagree BAM-BAM, I don't know exactly where you get that cops are self regulated. Not to get into a battle over semantics. Cops are doing a job, just like any other. Their job happens to be one of extremely high profile, and high risk. And sometimes, like any job, they have to make a choice on the spot. It's not like a job at MacDonalds, where the kid has a minute to decide if the order called for large fries, or super-sized fries. Usually the cops have to decided in an instant, and their lives depend on the choice. Like has been said here several times, the kids was told to stop, and was WARNED. What the hell did he expect? I've had run ins with cops before. In my youth, I was in plenty of trouble. And even today, my Fiero keeps me in the limelight with law enforcement. The ONLY time I've ever had a cop draw his gun on me was when I ran (I was on foot). Now, unless I'm a hell of alot smarter most (which maybe I am, considering the topic of this thread) I know it only took me about a millionth of a second to decide that since this cop had drawn his gun, and pointed it at me, he must mean business. I surrendered quicker than you can blink. I did exactly as I was instructed, and guess what, no shots were fired. I'm alive to tell the tale. I also learned, I never wanted to be on the business end of a firearm ever again. Maybe this kid was stupid, but more likely, he was just plain defiant, and basically "no balls"-ing the cop to shoot his ass. Either way, he was responsible for his actions. If he had done what he was told, he would be alive today. I can only reiterate some of the things that have been said here, that seem to be getting brushed under the carpet. The statistics regarding the number of shootings are skewed, since they do not mention how many whites have been shot. They also don't talk about the injuries and deaths incurred by cops in those shootings. No one here has mentioned that one of those incidents, was where a kid was pulled over, and tried to drive away. The cop reached into the car to shut off the ignition and got hung up. The kid drove and dragged the cop to his death, but before he died, he was able to pull his weapon and shoot the kid in the head. How do we expect law enforcement to deal with a society of criminals who so obviously place their own freedom, and unwillingness to accept responsibiltiy for their actions to the extent that they place those things above the very lives of others with such blatant disregard. Those who think cops should automatically be discharged or punished if they should shoot a criminal in the commission of a crime, (and running from the police IS a crime of it's own) even when there's only the indication that there is a hostile gesture, such as pulling a gun, obviously have no clue what it's like for cops, and have no desire to try and understand. <BR>As for the rioting. Oh brother, give me a break. What's the point. To me that's nothing more than a bunch of idiots looking for an excuse to loot and destroy. I wonder how much of the property they are stealing in the name of the "victim" will end up in the hands of the "victim"'s family, or how that family will benefit from these actions. Yeah, I bet those rioters are doing it looking for justice. Right. Dumb. Just dumb.||||reg||1
Z||000059||Formula88||04-20-2001||09:41 AM||||I haven't completely digested everyone's replies, yet, but I have one simple point to add to the race argument.<P>If a black man says "Black power!" it's considered ethnic pride.  If a white man says "White power!" it's considered racism.  As long as people of all color continue to perpetuate this type of double standard there will be racism.<P>An interesting side note.  It's amazing how much appearance changes how people look at you.  I'm a mid 30's white male.  I decided to shave my head to go for a new look.  I think it looks rather professional myself, but ever since then I have noticed store security and other people keeping a closer eye on me.  If I'm dressed casual (especially when I'm wearing my leather Harley jacket - and no, I'm not a biker), people tend to give me a wide berth, but they also keep a closer watch on me.  I know it's different, but it does open your eyes to people's perception of you based solely on one aspect of your appearance.  ||||reg||6
Z||000060||BAM-BAM||04-22-2001||03:53 PM||||  The "CRAP" the police are self regulated is FACT! First the Police have there internal Affairs Division that looks into any suspected inproprieties that may befall a "Fellow" officer. Second, in the case of a shooting death involving a police officer, there is the ever famous "Coronors Inquest". The is the cicus where "Fellow" officers and only a select few witnesses are invited to detemine plausability. In the afore mentioned case of the retarted Hispanic man standing in his driveway with a hunting knife, surrounded by police officers. You mean to tell me that there was no other viable solution than to put eight rounds into him, CENTER MASS!! Also, in the case of the French tourist that was beaten to death in the Clark County Detention Center, He was in a holding tank for drunk and disorderly. He was choked and beaten. Video shows that when he began to l ose conciousness and fell to the floor one of our "Boys in Blue" felt it neccessary to stand on his neck to prevent any further outbursts. Needless to say that in both cases IA found the officers justified as did the Coroners Inquest. You show me wher there was a split second decision made. I realize what the police are up against. But lets face it, fewer police die due to violence in a ten year period than do electrical workers, truckdrivers, or oilfield workers, in a single year. I'm not talking all combigned, I mean individualy. Not all police are bad, but they do enjoy a sense of protection from prosecution in the event that they make the wrong decision. Oh, and one more thing, They are internaly regulated. <P>Late!<P>------------------<BR>Knowledge is POWER!<BR>That is why the ignorant remain Weak!||||reg||1
